Subject: [PATCH] feat: rename to Ananse Comms Lab and use the real logo
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

THE NAME

Every place a person reads it: the header and footer, the page title and
social preview cards, the About and Privacy pages, the certificate and its
public verification page, and the footer of every email the app sends. Also
the two places it is said to a machine rather than a reader — the default
Brevo sender name, and the line in the AI drafting prompt that tells the
model whose coursework it is writing.

The domain stays energycommslab.africa, so nothing in Clerk, Railway or DNS
is touched. The workspace folder and package keep the old name: no reader
ever sees them, and renaming would mean re-pointing Railway's build, the API
server's lookup of the web build, and the TypeScript project references —
real risk for no visible gain.

THE LOGO

The wordmark was set as text in a bold sans, which is not the logo. Four
assets are now cut from the one coloured original supplied.

The white footer version is generated from that same file rather than taken
from the white mockup, which is a presentation piece on a grey gradient with
a glow behind it — dropping it in would have put a grey panel in the footer.
The alpha channel is recovered from the artwork's distance from its white
paper, so the logo sits on the cream header and the near-black footer with no
box around it. Ink colours are carried through untouched, so the greens, rust
and gold stay exactly as drawn.

On a phone the header shows the woven mark alone: the full lockup is nearly
three times as wide as it is tall, and at mobile header height "COMMS LAB"
would be a few pixels of grey mush.

Two placeholders went with it. The favicon was a plain orange rounded square,
which told a crowded row of tabs nothing. And Clerk's sign-in card pointed at
the old text-only logo.svg, so the first screen every facilitator sees showed
the least finished version of the brand.

Images declare width and height so the header does not jolt as they load, and
each carries the name as alt text, the logo now being the only thing that
names the site to a screen reader.

